# Patent Claims

## Claim 1 (Independent System Claim)

A system for controlling individual light sources using a mobile device, the system comprising:

a camera configured to capture an image of a light pattern generated by a plurality of light sources, wherein the light pattern comprises a plurality of markers, each marker corresponding to a respective one of the plurality of light sources;

a display coupled to the mobile device;

a processor communicatively coupled to the camera and the display; and

a non-transitory memory storing instructions that, when executed by the processor, cause the system to:

receive the image captured by the camera;

analyze the image to detect a position within the image of each of the plurality of markers;

determine, for each detected marker position, an identity of the corresponding light source among the plurality of light sources;

generate a graphical user interface comprising a plurality of interactive control elements, wherein each interactive control element is positioned at a display coordinate corresponding to the detected position of a respective marker;

render the graphical user interface as an overlay on a display of the captured image;

receive a user touch input at a location on the display;

determine that the location of the user touch input corresponds to one of the plurality of interactive control elements;

identify, based on the corresponding interactive control element, the light source associated with the touch input; and

transmit a control command to the identified light source to modify at least one operational parameter of the identified light source.

---

## Claim 2

The system of claim 1, wherein transmitting the control command comprises transmitting via a wireless communication protocol selected from the group consisting of Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Bluetooth Low Energy, Zigbee, and a proprietary mesh network protocol.

## Claim 3

The system of claim 1, wherein analyzing the image to detect the position of each marker comprises applying a blob detection algorithm to identify contiguous pixel regions exceeding a predetermined brightness or intensity threshold.

## Claim 4

The system of claim 1, wherein each marker is emitted at a distinguishable wavelength, color, modulation frequency, or temporal blink pattern unique to its corresponding light source, and wherein determining the identity of the corresponding light source comprises decoding the wavelength, color, modulation frequency, or blink pattern from the captured image.

## Claim 5

The system of claim 1, wherein the instructions further cause the system to perform a calibration procedure that generates and stores a mapping between each detected marker position or decoded marker identifier and a network address associated with the corresponding light source.

## Claim 6

The system of claim 1, wherein modifying the at least one operational parameter comprises adjusting one or more of brightness, color, color temperature, hue, saturation, or on/off state of the identified light source.

## Claim 7

The system of claim 1, wherein the graphical user interface further comprises, for each interactive control element, a status indicator representing a current brightness, color, or on/off state of the corresponding light source, the status indicator being updated based on state information received from the light source.

## Claim 8

The system of claim 1, wherein the instructions further cause the system to track the position of each marker across a plurality of successive image frames captured by the camera, and to update a position of each corresponding interactive control element in real time to maintain alignment between the graphical user interface overlay and the plurality of markers as the camera is moved.

## Claim 9

The system of claim 1, wherein receiving the user touch input comprises receiving a multi-touch gesture selecting two or more interactive control elements simultaneously, and wherein transmitting the control command comprises transmitting a group control command to the light sources corresponding to the two or more selected interactive control elements.

## Claim 10

The system of claim 1, wherein the plurality of light sources comprise addressable stage lighting fixtures, and wherein the control command is formatted according to a DMX512, Art-Net, or sACN lighting control protocol.

## Claim 11

The system of claim 1, wherein the instructions further cause the system to display, on the graphical user interface, a numerical or textual label proximate to each interactive control element, the label identifying a fixture number, channel, or zone associated with the corresponding light source.

## Claim 12

The system of claim 1, wherein detecting the position of each marker comprises applying a trained machine learning model to the captured image to classify pixel regions as markers and to estimate their respective positions.

---

## Claim 13 (Independent Method Claim)

A method for controlling individual light sources using a mobile device, the method comprising:

capturing, via a camera of the mobile device, an image of a light pattern generated by a plurality of light sources, the light pattern comprising a plurality of markers each corresponding to a respective one of the plurality of light sources;

analyzing the captured image to detect a position within the image of each of the plurality of markers;

determining, for each detected marker position, an identity of the corresponding light source;

generating a graphical user interface comprising a plurality of interactive control elements, each positioned at a display coordinate corresponding to a detected marker position;

displaying the graphical user interface as an overlay atop a display of the captured image;

receiving a touch input at a location on the display of the mobile device;

determining that the location of the touch input corresponds to a particular interactive control element among the plurality of interactive control elements;

identifying, based on the particular interactive control element, the light source associated with the touch input; and

transmitting, from the mobile device, a control command to the identified light source to modify an operational parameter thereof.

## Claim 14

The method of claim 13, further comprising, prior to analyzing the captured image, performing a calibration operation to establish a correspondence between marker positions and light source network identifiers.

## Claim 15

The method of claim 13, wherein transmitting the control command comprises wirelessly transmitting the control command directly to the identified light source or indirectly via an intermediate lighting controller or gateway device.

---

## Claim 16 (Independent Computer-Readable Medium Claim)

A non-transitory computer-readable storage medium storing instructions that, when executed by a processor of a mobile device, cause the mobile device to:

receive an image captured by a camera of the mobile device, the image depicting a light pattern comprising a plurality of markers generated by a plurality of light sources;

detect, within the captured image, a position corresponding to each of the plurality of markers;

determine an identity of a light source corresponding to each detected marker position;

generate and display a graphical user interface overlay on the captured image, the overlay comprising a plurality of touch-selectable control elements positioned at the detected marker positions;

receive a touch input selecting one of the touch-selectable control elements;

determine the light source corresponding to the selected control element; and

transmit a control signal to the determined light source to adjust an operational parameter of the light source.

## Claim 17

The non-transitory computer-readable storage medium of claim 16, wherein the instructions further cause the mobile device to display, in response to the touch input, an expanded control panel comprising selectable options for adjusting brightness, color, and on/off state of the determined light source.

# INDEPENDENT APPARATUS CLAIM

**Claim 1.** A user device for controlling a lighting system, the user device comprising:

a display configured to present a graphical user interface;

a camera interface configured to communicatively couple with a camera and receive image data therefrom;

a communication interface configured to transmit control signals to one or more lamps of the lighting system, each lamp comprising a light source configured to emit a light pattern comprising a plurality of markers; and

a processor operatively coupled to the display, the camera interface, and the communication interface, the processor configured to execute an application stored in a memory of the user device, wherein execution of the application causes the processor to:

transmit, via the communication interface, a lamp control signal to activate at least one light source of the lighting system to emit the light pattern comprising the plurality of markers;

receive, via the camera interface, a captured image of the light pattern, the captured image comprising a distorted representation of the light pattern resulting from a projection of the light pattern onto a surface within a field of view of the camera;

detect, within the distorted representation of the light pattern, a position of each marker of the plurality of markers to obtain a set of detected marker positions;

determine, based on the set of detected marker positions, a spatial mapping between the distorted representation of the light pattern and the graphical user interface presented on the display; and

generate and align, based on the spatial mapping, a control overlay on the graphical user interface, the control overlay comprising one or more interactive control elements positioned in correspondence with the set of detected marker positions, such that user interaction with the one or more interactive control elements on the display causes the processor to transmit, via the communication interface, a corresponding lighting control signal to the lighting system.
